---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About NO BALL GAMES | From Restricted Spaces to Street Culture Brand"
  description="Learn the story behind NO BALL GAMES, the Charlie Buster / Grime Art layer, and the sign-flip street-culture world growing into lore and digital collectibles."
  current="about"
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <span class="eyebrow">About the brand</span>
    <h1>From a warning sign to a creative weapon.</h1>
    <p>
      NO BALL GAMES starts with a simple British street image: a sign telling people what they cannot do. The brand flips that message, then builds out through Charlie Buster's visual language, collector culture and a growing lore archive.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section split-grid">
    <article class="panel-card">
      <span class="kicker">The meaning</span>
      <h2>Denied space becomes owned space.</h2>
      <p>
        The sign is supposed to shut down play. In this brand, it becomes the opposite: a reminder to create even when the space was never designed for you.
      </p>
      <p>
        NO BALL GAMES stands for people who grew up around hard concrete, rules, warnings, locked courts, blank walls and public spaces that felt controlled by everyone except the people living there.
      </p>
      <a class="inline-link" href={`${base}lore/`}>Read the lore archive →</a>
    </article>

    <article class="panel-card">
      <span class="kicker">Artist layer</span>
      <h2>Charlie Buster shapes the visual backbone.</h2>
      <p>
        The current site positions Charlie Buster as the creative force behind the brand direction, with Grime Art acting as the raw visual language that feeds the walls, signs, clothing and portrait-led edge of NO BALL GAMES.
      </p>
      <div class="coming-soon-tag">Source-needed history stays in research</div>
      <ul class="list-clean">
        <li>Graffiti attitude without fake polish.</li>
        <li>British estate culture and urban memory.</li>
        <li>Grime Art framing that should be described carefully when proof is still being gathered.</li>
        <li>Digital-first culture without losing the real-world edge.</li>
        <li>A brand system that can expand into art, games, clothing, lore and XRPL-side concepts.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Origin timeline</h2>
      <p>How the sign became a brand system.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="timeline">
      <article class="timeline-item">
        <strong>Start</strong>
        <div>
          <h3>The sign exists everywhere</h3>
          <p>Yellow enamel plates, painted concrete warnings, stencilled fences. The phrase is part of British urban landscape — instantly recognisable, rarely questioned.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="timeline-item">
        <strong>Flip</strong>
        <div>
          <h3>Restriction becomes identity</h3>
          <p>The decision: take the warning, own the phrase, build a brand around the feeling of being told where not to be. The ban is the badge.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="timeline-item">
        <strong>Build</strong>
        <div>
          <h3>Charlie Buster and Grime Art add texture</h3>
          <p>The artist layer introduces painted jackets, portrait work, graffiti texture and Grime Art positioning. Historical founder claims should still be checked against the truth archive before they are presented as fact.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="timeline-item">
        <strong>Expand</strong>
        <div>
          <h3>Lore, collectibles and XRPL branches</h3>
          <p>The brand extends into digital collectibles, a story universe, collector drops, gaming concepts and the XRP KIDs branch with Alpha Aaron, FRET and Block Buster OG. <a class="inline-link" href={`${base}xrp/`}>See the XRP layer →</a></p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Brand rules</h2>
      <p>The site should feel clean enough to trust and raw enough to believe.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="kicker">Rule one</span>
        <h3>No fake corporate voice</h3>
        <p>Keep the language direct, visual and culturally grounded. The brand should sound like it came from the wall, not a boardroom.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="kicker">Rule two</span>
        <h3>Real culture first</h3>
        <p>Art, people, places, stories and community matter more than empty hype. Digital tools should support the culture, not replace it.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="kicker">Rule three</span>
        <h3>Truth labels matter</h3>
        <p>Use verified facts when they exist. Use internal-canon and source-needed wording when the archive is still being built. That is how the public site stays believable.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section split-grid">
    <article class="panel-card">
      <span class="kicker">Explore further</span>
      <h2>Go deeper into the world.</h2>
      <p>The brand has multiple layers. Start with the projects, dig into the lore, browse the collections or connect with the community.</p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}projects/`}>View projects</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href={`${base}community/`}>Join community</a>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article class="panel-card">
      <span class="kicker">Quick links</span>
      <ul class="list-clean">
        <li><a class="inline-link" href={`${base}lore/`}>Lore archive — characters, signs and restricted zones</a></li>
        <li><a class="inline-link" href={`${base}collections/`}>Collections — physical art, clothing and digital drops</a></li>
        <li><a class="inline-link" href={`${base}xrp/`}>XRP page — XRP KIDs, FRET and Block Buster OG</a></li>
        <li><a class="inline-link" href={`${base}research/`}>Research vault — truth files and source material</a></li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
